<Prose title="How this works" active="method" description="Every charity is a model file that reproduces its evaluator's numbers, re-ranked by values you set, in years of healthy life per $1,000.">
  <h2>Models are files you can read</h2>
  <p>
    Each charity is one file in a public git repository. A model is a list of rows, like a spreadsheet: inputs with a value, and calculated rows with a formula over other rows. Every input says what kind of number it is (<strong>observed</strong> in data, an <strong>estimate</strong> made elsewhere, or a <strong>judgment</strong>), where it comes from and why it has the value it has. The file's history is the model's changelog.
  </p>
  <p>
    Models imported from an evaluator have to reproduce that evaluator's own numbers: every calculated row records the value the original spreadsheet shows, and the build fails if the model drifts from it. Where we disagree with a source, we say so rather than quietly changing its numbers.
  </p>

  <h2 id="healthy-years">One unit: years of healthy life</h2>
  <p>
    Every result is in <strong>years of healthy life per $1,000 donated</strong>. A year of healthy life is one person living one more year in full health; researchers call it a DALY averted. Evaluators count good in different units (GiveWell in years of doubled income, animal analyses in days of pain, the Happier Lives Institute in life satisfaction), so we convert each into healthy years at exchange rates you can change under Your values, beside the rankings. GiveDirectly's cash transfers come to about 4.
  </p>
  <h3 id="animals">Animals</h3>
  <p>
    Every animal model takes the same steps after the evaluator's own result. Its days of pain, with milder pain counted as a fraction of a day, are split by species. Each species is counted in chickens, by Rethink Priorities' estimates of how much it can suffer relative to a chicken: a pig about 1.6 chickens, a farmed fish about 0.2, a shrimp about 0.1. That gives <strong>chicken-equivalent pain days</strong>, which become healthy years through two of your values: how a chicken's suffering compares with a person's (33% by default), and how much healthy life a day of disabling pain costs (about 10 hours). ACE's figures in suffering-adjusted days already carry Ambitious Impact's own species weights; we take those out first, so the evaluator's view still reproduces ACE.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where ACE's models use different numbers for the same change, such as a hen moved out of a cage or a meal replaced, ours use one shared figure, and the row says so: "changed for consistency", with ACE's own number beside it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rates that rest on evidence, such as how life satisfaction compares with income, are fixed; they and the data several models share are listed, with sources, under <a href="/data">shared data</a>. Only value judgments are left for you to set.
  </p>

  <h2>Credit for shared outcomes</h2>
  <p>
    Most outcomes are paid for by more than one funder. By default each model uses its evaluator's counterfactual logic, such as GiveWell's adjustments for money a grant pulls in from governments or pushes elsewhere. Switch to <strong>credit by share of resources</strong> and an outcome is shared out by everything that went into it: every funder's money, in-kind costs, and staff's pay below what they could earn elsewhere. Shares add up to 100%, so nothing is counted twice.
  </p>

  <h2>Ranges</h2>
  <p>
    Inputs carry a low, best and high value, taken from the source wherever it gives one. The dot on each bar uses every best guess. The bar runs from every input at the end that lowers the result to every input at the end that raises it, so it is deliberately wide, not a 90% interval. Rankings use the dot.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each charity's page shows where the uncertainty comes from: which inputs move the result most, and how loosely each is known.
  </p>

  <h2>Caveats</h2>
  <p>
    A warning sign marks a charity when its range is very wide (over 100×), when most of its uncertainty comes from judgment calls, or when the analysis it rests on is more than 18 months old.
  </p>

  <h2 id="imported-models">Imported models</h2>
  <p>
    Most models are evaluators' spreadsheets imported whole: every public model GiveWell links from its grant pages, plus HLI's and ACE's (the <a href="https://github.com/heychristoph/charityevals/tree/main/inventory">inventory</a> lists what we found and why anything was left out). Each sheet is traced from its bottom line, every cell it depends on becomes a row, and each calculated row must reproduce the sheet's value. Shared values such as the value of a life and the discount rate are then tied to your values, and statistics several sheets share live once as shared data.
  </p>

  <h2>What this is not</h2>
  <p>
    Not an evaluator's recommendation, and not endorsed by GiveWell, Animal Charity Evaluators or any charity listed. Cost-effectiveness is one input to a giving decision; room for more funding, organisational strength and track record matter too and aren't modelled here yet.
  </p>
</Prose>
